<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>A sentence was a relay race.</h2><p>A recurrent network updates a running memory one word at a time. Training on long sequences meant waiting for earlier steps. Attention already let translation models look back at source words, but recurrence still shaped the architecture.</p><p>Imagine passing a message down a line of people. Each person can add something, but the person at the end has to wait. Could the words talk directly instead?</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Let words look at words.</h2><p>The 2017 Transformer replaced recurrence and convolution (mixing nearby positions) with attention and small networks applied at each position. <strong>Self-attention</strong> means positions in the same sequence read information from one another.</p><p>Picture a library desk. A <strong>query</strong> is what you are looking for; a <strong>key</strong> is a label to match against; a <strong>value</strong> is the information you take away. Attention scores the matches, turns them into weights, and mixes the values. Several <strong>heads</strong> perform different learned reads in parallel.</p><p><strong>Positional encodings</strong> add information about word order. During training, the decoder—the part that writes the output—can process known target words in parallel while hiding later words. Generating a new translation still proceeds one token at a time; a <strong>token</strong> is a chunk of text.</p>
