<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Change the question.<br />Change the read.</h2><p>Try moving from “sat” toward “cat.” Then hide future words. Which bars disappear, and where does their share go?</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to use the toy. Its read assigns each visible word a share of the attention, then averages their value numbers using those shares.</p></noscript><AttentionToy client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>A different way to build.</h2><p>The Transformer made attention the main route for exchanging information between positions. GPT later used Transformer decoders for pretraining: learn from ordinary text first, then adapt to a task.</p><p>Keep this picture: every word asks a question, other words offer information, and attention decides how much to take from each.</p><a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/pretraining/"><span>Next · 2018</span><strong>Pretrain, then fine-tune ↗</strong><span>Can reading first make a model easier to teach?</span></a></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>Equations, architecture, results & limits</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>One attention head</h3><p><code>Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QKᵀ / √dₖ + M)V</code></p><p>Q, K, and V are learned projections of the input representations. The dot product measures a query–key match; dₖ is the key dimension. Softmax turns scores into positive weights that sum to one. M is zero for allowed positions and negative infinity for hidden ones. The toy uses dₖ = 2.</p><h3>The full architecture</h3><p>The original base model has six encoder layers and six decoder layers, a model width of 512, eight attention heads, and feed-forward inner width 2,048. Each layer combines attention, position-wise feed-forward networks, residual connections (adding a sublayer’s input back to its output), and layer normalization (rescaling a representation’s features). The decoder adds attention over encoder outputs. The paper uses sine and cosine positional encodings.</p><h3>Headline result</h3><p>The big model reported 28.4 BLEU on WMT 2014 English-to-German translation. BLEU compares translated text with human reference translations; higher is better on the same evaluation. Its reported training run took 3.5 days on eight P100 GPUs.</p><h3>Limits of the idea</h3><p>Dense self-attention forms a score for every pair of positions, so its score matrix grows quadratically with sequence length. Direct paths do not guarantee correct understanding. The paper studied translation and parsing; the architecture by itself does not establish chatbot reliability.</p></div></details>
